1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,000
Support teams handle hundreds of
incidents every day.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,400
Most were solved before.
The knowledge just isn't connected.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:12,400
So the same fault gets
diagnosed twice.

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:16,400
One hundred and four records,
from four real systems.

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,400
The service desk. SAP.
SharePoint. And the knowledge base.

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,000
Every record goes through
three checks before it's stored.

7
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:27,200
Restricted and personal data
never enters the index.

8
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,400
Now Find a Solution.
You type the problem.

9
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:35,800
Matches rank by how well
they've worked, not just wording.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,800
Add the incident detail. Error code,
module, and environment.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,800
Three badges light up.
It matched this ticket exactly.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Now the interesting part.
Same code, test environment.

13
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,400
A different root cause.
That's the trap it avoids.

14
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:57,400
The same search crosses languages.
One question, three languages.

15
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,400
English, Malay, Chinese,
and now Japanese.

16
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:06,000
Translate the Chinese one
and it's the same fix.

17
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,600
So any team's knowledge
stays reusable across the company.

18
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:13,400
Knowledge from any team,
in any language.

19
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,800
This is the vocabulary
a consultant actually uses.

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,400
Production order won't create.
Twelve out of twelve worked.

21
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:26,400
Now the same category,
a record that failed before.

22
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:30,000
Seven of nine is too low.
So it declines.

23
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,900
Same category, different
record history.

24
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,000
Chat is the second way in.

25
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:41,200
I forgot my password.
It answers directly.

26
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:45,400
Worked forty-five out of forty-five.

27
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:50,000
Now something vaguer.
A finance user cannot open it.

28
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:54,000
It refuses to commit.
The check is not satisfied.

29
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:58,000
Notice what it doesn't do.
No numbers, no sources.

30
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:02,600
Then I add what I know.
The code, the module, production.

31
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:06,800
Now it commits.
Same question, better context.

32
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,400
Nothing changed but the
evidence I gave it.

33
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:14,200
What if the fix doesn't work?

34
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:18,600
I tell it the email fix failed.
It rules it out.

35
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:23,200
Then it offers the next best
match. Outlook syncing.

36
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:27,600
A real conversation,
not a search box.

37
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:32,600
It remembers what already failed,
so it doesn't offer it again.

38
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,000
And this isn't only
for troubleshooting.

39
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:41,400
I ask for a new starter's
laptop procedure.

40
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:45,800
It finds the official standard
procedure document.

41
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:51,000
Finally, something it
should not answer.

42
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:55,200
I run out of milk.
It says no match exists.

43
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:59,400
That refusal is the feature.

44
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:04,000
Two ways in. Both transparent.

45
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:07,400
Both say when they don't know.
